// A generic machine's `satisfies` edge is settled on the universal template,
// where its static telescope (one type parameter) cannot correspond to a
// concrete operator requirement's empty telescope, so no exact overload is
// retained. `Provider<Element>` is then instantiated, and the instance's own
// entry signature `(u64) -> u64` selects `Policy::convert` exactly while the
// cloned conformance still carries the unsettled selection. The instance must
// therefore reject with the specialized realization's exact-selection rule
// (checking/src/declarations/traits/conformance/machine_conformance.rs:661):
//
//   machine `Provider$specialized$<digest>$0` operator realization
//   `Policy::convert` does not retain the exact selected overload
//
// The instantiation is the trigger: the same program without the
// `Provider<u64>(3)` call reports only the template's own refusal. That second
// line is part of the same rejection and must be read with it, not instead of
// it -- a fragment check would pass on either line alone, but only the pair
// tells the story:
//
//   machine `Provider` does not match one exact overload of operator
//   requirement `Policy::convert`; its entry parameter and result types must
//   equal one declared requirement signature
//
// The template line is already pinned by
// fail/providers/module_operator_satisfier_signature_mismatch and
// fail/operators/result_domain_operator_family_wrong_signature; this fixture
// is the only pin of the specialized realization's line.
data Policy { }
boundary operator Policy::convert(value: u64) -> u64;
machine Provider<Element>(value: Element) -> Element satisfies Policy::convert { value }
machine main() {
    let result: u64 = Provider<u64>(3);
}
